Investors seem to be nervous, unsure of what to take away from the Fed statement this week and not bullish enough to push the market strongly higher despite a bumper earnings season. Meanwhile, trade tensions with China have bubbled back to the surface, and investors are looking toward an Forget The $2.9 Million Mickey Mantle Card, There Are Three Worth $10 Million
&l;p&g;&l;img class=&q;size-full wp-image-5101&q; src=&q;http://blogs-images.forbes.com/davidseideman/files/2018/04/resizedmantle.jpg?width=960&q; alt=&q;&q; data-height=&q;1095&q; data-width=&q;660&q;&g; Ken Kendrick&s;s gem mint Mantle.
&l;/p&g;&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;Whether or not you&a;rsquo;re a baseball card collector, you&a;rsquo;re probably aware of the recent sale of a mint 1952 Mickey Mantle Topps baseball card for $2.88 million in &l;a href=&q;https://sports.ha.com/itm/baseball/1952-topps-mickey-mantle-311-psa-mint-9/a/50003-80477.s?ic4=GalleryView-Thumbnail-071515&q; target=&q;_blank&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s2&q;&g;Heritage&a;rsquo;s auction&l;/span&g;&l;/a&g;. The PSA 9, on a scale of one to ten, set a new record for a post-war card and was ten times more than the last publicly sold mint &a;rsquo;52 Topps Mantle, which changed hands for $282,588 in 2006.&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Heritage&a;rsquo;s celebrity seller is ex-lineman and &l;a href=&q;https://www.forbes.com/sites/davidseideman/2018/03/06/the-mystery-mickey-mantle-card-owner-is-a-popular-super-bowl-champion/#c3d820966622&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s2&q;&g;Super Bowl champion Evan Mathis&l;/span&g;&l;/a&g;.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;But the bigger, untold story is that are three &l;b&g;gem mint&l;/b&g; PSA 10 &a;rsquo;52 Topps Mantles which are twice as rare–three versus six–and that they may well be worth $10 million each, according to six heavy hitters whom I consult regularly. (See &a;ldquo;Sources&a;rdquo; below).&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;So the question is, if you had the cash, could you buy the card? It would depend on the three owners.&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;1)&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Leading off is hobby pioneer and Colorado attorney Marshall Fogel. He got in on the ground floor of&l;a href=&q;https://www.psacard.com/articles/articleview/7813/pulp-non-fiction-famous-1952-topps-baseball-find&q; target=&q;_blank&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s2&q;&g; Al &a;ldquo;Mr. Mint&a;rdquo; Rosen&a;rsquo;s find in 1986 in&l;/span&g;&l;/a&g; the Boston area. It consisted of a case of pristine 1952 Topps cards that had sat in a truck driver&a;rsquo;s attic for decades. &l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;&q;This was one of the greatest finds in hobby history,&q; Fogel told PSA. &q;I owned two PSA 9s of Mantle from that find, and I sold those later to help pay for my PSA 10 Mantle.&a;rdquo;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s3&q;&g;&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;The original asking price for a &a;ldquo;sharp&a;rdquo; Mantle was $3,500.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;In 1996, Fogel paid $121,000 for his gem mint specimen. A frequent guest on TV, he enjoys the limelight too much to leave it.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;img class=&q;size-full wp-image-5079&q; src=&q;http://blogs-images.forbes.com/davidseideman/files/2018/04/1952_Topps_baseball_penny_pack__1524098538_45282-3.jpg?width=960&q; alt=&q;&q; data-height=&q;396&q; data-width=&q;660&q;&g; An unopened 1952 Topps pack.
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;2)&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;The second owner of a gem mint Mantle is Ken Kendrick, the affable managing general partner of the Arizona Diamondbacks. In 2016, the Phoenix Art Museum mounted an exhibit of his mind-blowing collection. &a;ldquo; 16 of the top 20 rated sports trading cards in the world, along with an additional 25 highly valued cards,&a;rdquo;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s3&q;&g;&l;a href=&q;https://www.sportscollectorsdaily.com/diamondbacks-collection-on-exhibit/&q; target=&q;_blank&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s4&q;&g;reported Rich Mueller&l;/span&g;&l;/a&g;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;, &l;i&g;Sports Collector&a;rsquo;s Daily&l;/i&g; editor. &l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Kendrick&a;rsquo;s Mantle once&a;nbsp;belonged to ex-big league pitcher Tom Candiotti. He paid $104,500 for it in 1998. In 2005, Candiotti publicized his card and the rest of his collection in a &l;a href=&q;https://www.psacard.com/articles/articleview/4390/tom-candiotti-best-collection&q; target=&q;_blank&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s2&q;&g;PSA story&l;/span&g;&l;/a&g;.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Besides the gem mint Mantle, Kendrick owns the highest graded Honus Wagner; a PSA 8, which cost him $2.8 million in 2009. &l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;The most valuable baseball card of all is the PSA 5 Jumbo Wagner, $3.12 million. The Mathis Mantle is now the second most valuable.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Like Fogel, Kendrick derives immense pleasure from periodically putting his Mantle and other crown jewels on display.&l;span class=&q;Apple-converted-space&q;&g;&a;nbsp; &l;/span&g;His Mantle isn&a;rsquo;t going anywhere, either.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;3)&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;The third Mantle is a wild card.&a;nbsp;&a;nbsp;&l;/span&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;&a;ldquo;The collector is a very private guy,&a;rdquo; says Joe Orlando, president of PSA, who has spoken to him several times.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;img class=&q;size-full wp-image-4903&q; src=&q;http://blogs-images.forbes.com/davidseideman/files/2018/03/mantleside.jpg?width=960&q; alt=&q;&q; data-height=&q;660&q; data-width=&q;1116&q;&g; The back of the PSA 9 Mantle sold by Heritage.
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Therefore, when it comes to the three Mantles all bets may be off.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;&a;ldquo;There&a;rsquo;s no telling how much one could sell for now, with the Mantle card craze in full force, not to mention the general phenomenon of individuals and corporations paying astronomical figures for sports memorabilia,&a;rdquo; Wilen wrote in his 2016 book.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Rick Snyder doubts that even $10 million will seal the deal. &a;ldquo;I&a;rsquo;ve heard that one of the owners was offered $10 million and passed,&a;rdquo; said the owner of &l;a href=&q;https://mintstateinc.com/&q; target=&q;_blank&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s2&q;&g;MINT State&l;/span&g;&l;/a&g; and broker of the $3 million Lucky 7 Ty Cobb Find. &l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;The rejection didn&a;rsquo;t surprise him. &a;ldquo;The owners can&a;rsquo;t replace the item,&a;rdquo; Snyder added. &a;ldquo;The baseball card becomes almost priceless like a piece of art. I think the Mantle could go for more than $10 million.&a;rdquo; &l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
&l;p class=&q;p1&q;&g;&l;span class=&q;s1&q;&g;Sources: Chris Ivy, director of Heritage&a;rsquo;s sports division; Josh Evans, founder of Lelands; Brent Huigens, CEO of PWCC; Leighton Sheldon, owner of Just Collect: Rick Snyder, owner MINT State; and Don Hontz, an independent dealer.&l;/span&g;&l;/p&g;
